I can already see the problem. 👍

The issue is that you're showing the modal, but you're not stopping the original form submission.

Inside a submit event listener, simply doing:

return;
does not prevent the browser from submitting the form.

You must call:

e.preventDefault();
before showing the modal.

Replace this section
// Confirm only Bulk Delete
if (bulkAction.value === "delete") {

    const deleteModal = new bootstrap.Modal(
        document.getElementById("deleteModal")
    );

    document.getElementById("deleteMessage").textContent =
        `Are you sure you want to delete ${selected.length} selected prediction(s)?`;

    const deleteForm = document.getElementById("deleteForm");

    deleteForm.onsubmit = function (e) {

        e.preventDefault();

        bulkForm.submit();

    };

    deleteModal.show();

    return;
}
with:

// Confirm only Bulk Delete
if (bulkAction.value === "delete") {

    // STOP the form from submitting immediately
    e.preventDefault();

    const deleteModal = new bootstrap.Modal(
        document.getElementById("deleteModal")
    );

    document.getElementById("deleteMessage").textContent =
        `Are you sure you want to delete ${selected.length} selected prediction(s)?`;

    const deleteForm = document.getElementById("deleteForm");

    deleteForm.onsubmit = function (event) {

        event.preventDefault();

        deleteModal.hide();

        // Submit the original bulk form
        bulkForm.submit();

    };

    deleteModal.show();

    return;
}
However...
There is one more thing I want to verify before you test again.

Please paste your deleteModal.ejs (the reusable confirmation modal).

I specifically want to confirm it contains:

<form id="deleteForm">
and

<p id="deleteMessage"></p>
If those IDs don't match the JavaScript, the modal won't behave correctly.

One more observation
We're very close, but I think we can improve this architecture.

Right now, you have two different delete systems:

Single delete buttons.

Bulk delete.

I recommend making both use the exact same reusable modal and confirmation logic. That way, there is only one confirmation mechanism to maintain, and any future styling or behavior changes happen in one place. Once we get the bulk delete working, I'd like to refactor both flows to share the same confirmation system. That will make Prof Predict cleaner and easier to maintain.


